# PrivacyBuddy: source-available, not open source

This repository is public so that the organizers and judges of the Global x402 Challenge, and anyone else, can read the code. Public is not the same as open source. Nothing here gives you the right to copy, change, redistribute, host or sell the software, except as the terms below say.

The license is the **PolyForm Strict License 1.0.0**, printed in full and unchanged further down, plus the ChainAim terms in this section. Where a ChainAim term adds a permission or a limit, it applies on top of the PolyForm text.

## ChainAim terms

1. **What is covered.** Everything in this repository except the parts listed in term 6. In the PolyForm text below, "the software" means that.
2. **Judging and evaluation.** Reading the code, running it on your own computer and testing it, in order to evaluate it, is a permitted purpose. That includes judging the Global x402 Challenge, and it holds even when the evaluator works for a company. Putting the software into a product or service does not count as evaluating it.
3. **No hosting for others.** You may run the software only on your own computers, for yourself. You may not run it, or any part of it, as a service that other people can use over a network, free or paid, even for a noncommercial purpose, unless ChainAim has allowed it in writing.
4. **GitHub.** GitHub's Terms of Service let you view and fork this public repository on GitHub, and nothing here limits that. A fork stays under these terms and gives you no further rights.
5. **Trademarks.** PrivacyBuddy and ChainAim, and their logos, are unregistered trademarks of ChainAim. This license grants no right to use them. You may use the names only to say, accurately, that you are talking about this project.
6. **What is not covered.** `packages/route-engine/` is a fork of BlockRunAI/router-core and stays under the MIT License in its own `LICENSE` and `NOTICE` files. Packages installed from npm or elsewhere stay under their own licenses.
7. **Other permissions.** For any use these terms do not allow, including commercial use, ask first by opening an issue at https://github.com/chainaimdev/chainaim-router/issues.

